cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Income per 40 hours: a household-hours-normalised measure of
  real earnings in the United States, 1975-2024
message: >-
  If you use this measure, the derived data, or the methodology,
  please cite it as below.
type: dataset
authors:
  - name: PendulHaos
    website: https://www.youtube.com/@pendulhaos
abstract: >-
  Standard income statistics count a household with two fulltime
  earners the same as a household with one, so they are not
  comparable across a period in which the number of earners per
  household changed. Income per 40 hours divides household
  earnings by household hours worked and expresses the result as
  what one full-time work-year returns, allowing households with
  different labour supply to be compared directly. Built from
  Current Population Survey ASEC microdata via IPUMS for income
  years 1975 through 2024, with full methodology, robustness
  testing and replication code.
keywords:
  - income
  - wages
  - labour supply
  - household economics
  - Current Population Survey
  - CPS ASEC
  - real earnings
license: CC-BY-4.0
date-released: '2026-09-22'
version: '1.0'
references:
  - type: database
    title: IPUMS CPS
    institution:
      name: University of Minnesota
    url: https://www.ipums.org
    notes: >-
      Source data provided by the US Census Bureau and the Bureau
      of Labor Statistics.
